/*! \page developer_backend_math_page Vector and Reduction Operations

\ref developer_backend_field_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_comm_page  (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

PSCF provides a set of templates for classes and functions that perform 
basic mathematical operations on arrays of numbers. The FFT class 
template, which performs discrete Fourier transforms, was discussed on the 
\ref developer_backend_field_page "previous page".  This page discusses 
operations that we refer to as vector operations and reductions:

   - <b> Vector operations </b> take one or more arrays as inputs and 
     produce a single array as an output, such that each element of the 
     output array is computed from corresponding elements of the input 
     arrays.  Vector operations are performed by functions defined in 
     namespace Pscf::VecOp.

   - <b> Reductions </b>: take one or more arrays as inputs 
     and return a scalar output. Reductions are performed by functions 
     defined in namespace Pscf::Reduce.


Vector operations include vector-vector addition, vector-scalar 
multiplication, and elementwise vector-vector multiplication. 
Reduction operations include calculation of a dot product of two arrays 
and identification of the minimum or maximum elements of an array. 

For each function name that is defined in the Pscf::VecOp and Pscf::Reduce
namespaces, overloaded versions are designed to be used with different 
backends. Versions that are designed to be used with different backends 
are distinguished by the types used for array-valued function parameters:

   - Functions that used with a CUDA backend take array parameters that
     are specializations of Pscf::DeviceArray \<Data, Pscf::CUT \>, with 
     Data = cudaReal for arrays with real elements and Data = cudaComplex 
     for arrays with complex elements.

   - Functions that use a C++ backend instead take array parameters with 
     types that are specializations of Util::Array \<Data \>, with 
     Data = double for arrays with real elements and Data = fftw_complex 
     for arrays with complex elements.

These interfaces allow specializations of the RField, RFieldDft and CField 
class templates to be passed to such functions because the field class
specializations with a backend identifier class T=Pscf::CUT are all 
derived from specializations of DeviceArray \<Data, Pscf::CUT \>, while 
the field classes with T=PSCF::CPT are all indirectly derived from 
corresponding specializations of Util::Array \<Data\>. 

Vector operation and reduction functions that are designed for use with a 
CUDA backend are defined in files located in the src/pscf/backend/cuda 
directory. Corresponding functions that are designed for use with a C++
backend are defined in files located in the src/pscf/backend/cpp directory.

<b> Example </b>: 

The function Pscf::VecOp::addVV performs binary vector-vector addition. 
The file src/pscf/backend/cpp/VecOp.h declares a version of this function 
that uses a C++ backend to add two real arrays, which is declared within 
namespace Pscf::VecOp as:
\code
    void addVV(Array<double>& a, 
               Array<double> const & b, 
               Array<double> const & c);
\endcode
Here, Array<double> is used to denote Util::Array<double>.
The file src/pscf/backend/cuda/VecOp.h declares an overloaded version 
of this function that instead uses a Cuda backend, which is declared in 
namespace Pscf::VecOp as
\code
    void addVV(DeviceArray<cudaReal, CUT>& a, 
               DeviceArray<cudaReal, CUT> const & b, 
               DeviceArray<cudaReal, CUT> const & c);
\endcode
where DeviceArray<cudaReal, CUT> denotes 
Pscf::DeviceArray<cudaReal, Pscf::CUT>.
In interfaces for addVV and almost all other functions in namespace 
VecOp, the first function parameter is an output parameter that is used 
to return the result of a calculation, while the remaining parameters 
are input parameters.

PSCF relies on the C++ system for function overload resolution to find 
the correct overload of each of the functions defined in VecOp and 
Reduce namespaces, by inspecting the types of the arguments that are 
passed to the function. For example, suppose that the function 
Pscf::VecOp::addVV is used to add two real-valued arrays (or vectors) 
within a member function of a class template that takes a backend 
identifier class T as a template parameter. 
In specializations of that class template with T=Pscf::CPT, the addVV 
will always be passed arguments that are instances of subclasses of 
Util::Array<double>. In specializations with T=Pscf::CUT, the same
function invocation may be passed arguments that are instances of the 
class DeviceArray<cudaReal,CUT> or its subclasses.  The template type
system used by PSCF, in which different data types are used for arrays 
in specializations that use different backends, allows the compiler to 
choose the correct overload for functions defined in Pscf::VecOp and 
Pscf::Reduce when instantiating each such template specialization.

<b> Function Name Conventions </b>: 

The names of functions in namespace Pscf::VecOp follow a scheme in 
which function names usually starts with a shortened form of the name 
of a mathematical operation (e.g., "add" for addition or "mul" for 
multiplication) followed by a code that indicates the nature of the 
inputs (e.g. "VV" for an operations with two vector inputs or "VS" for 
an operation with a vector and scalar input).  The function name "addVV" 
thus indicates a function that adds to vectors, while "mulVS" is a 
function that performs vector-scalar multiplication. The string "Eq" 
can be added after the name of the binary operation to indicate 
functions in which the first argument is used as an input that is
modified to store the output, as done by the C compound arithmetic 
operators such as +=, *=, etc.  The function addEqV(a,b) thus yields 
an ouput array a for which a[i] += b[i] for each element with index 
i. 

See also:

\see Pscf_VecOp_Cuda_Module
\see Pscf_Reduce_Cuda_Module
\see Pscf_VecOp_Cpp_Module
\see Pscf_Reduce_Cpp_Module

